"dblp: Minji Hong (https://dblp.uni-trier.de/pid/308/3100.html)\nciteturn8720search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; CoRR abs/2509.19369 (2025)\nFamSearch: Visual Analysis of Genealogical Data. ISVC (2) 2021: 374-385\n\n## Informal and Other Publications\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2509-15658\n\nJisu Kim, Jinhee Park, Changhyun Jeon, Jungwoo Choi, Keonwoo Kim, Minji Hong, Sehyun Kim:\nChunk Knowledge Generation Model for Enhanced Information Retrieval: A Multi-task Learning Approach. CoRR abs/2509.15658 (2025)\n\nChanghyun Jeon, Jinhee Park, Jungwoo Choi, Keonwoo Kim, Jisu Kim, Minji Hong:\nSLM-Based Agentic AI with P-C-G: Optimized for Korean Tool Use. CoRR abs/2509.19369 (2025)\n\n## Coauthor Index\n\nsee FAQ\n\n  * What is the meaning of the colors in the coauthor index?\n  * How does dblp detect coauthor communities?\n\n1\n\nMichael Burch\n\n[c1]\n\n2\n\nJungwoo Choi\n\n[i2] [i1]\n\n3\n\nStasius Gladkis\n\n[c1]\n\n4\n\nChanghyun Jeon\n\n[i2] [i1]\n\n5\n\nJisu Kim\n\n[i2] [i1]\n\n6\n\nKeonwoo Kim\n\n[i2] [i1]\n\n7\n\nSehyun Kim\n\n[i2]\n\n8\n\nCarlo Lepelaars\n\n[c1]\n\n9\n\nJinhee Park\n\n[i2] [i1]\n\n10\n\nShahrukh Tufail\n\n[c1]\n\n11\n\nGünter Wallner\n\n[c1]\n\n12\n\nHuub van de Wetering\n\n[c1]\n\n13\n\nlast updated on 2025-11-22 05:21 CET by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-09-26&page=42&range=7)\nciteturn8720search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Sep 25 2025 cs.CL cs.AI arXiv:2509.19369v1\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2509.19405, author = {Tony Chahoud and Lorenzo Mario Amorosa and Riccardo Marini and Luca De Nardis}, title = {{I}mproving {O}utdoor {M}ulti-cell {F}ingerprinting-based {P}ositioning via {M}obile {D}ata {A}ugmentation}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2509.19405}, note = {arXiv:2509.19405v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nAccurate outdoor positioning in cellular networks is hindered by sparse, heterogeneous measurement collections and the high cost of exhaustive site surveys. This paper introduces a lightweight, modular mobile data augmentation framework designed to enhance multi-cell fingerprinting-based positioning using operator-collected minimization of drive test (MDT) records. The proposed approach decouples spatial and radio-feature synthesis: kernel density estimation (KDE) models the empirical spatial distribution to generate geographically coherent synthetic locations, while a k-nearest-neighbor (KNN)-based block produces augmented per-cell radio fingerprints. [Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2509.19403, author = {Sheng-Bin Duan and Jian-Long Hao and Tian-Yu Xiang and Xiao-Hu Zhou and Mei-Jiang Gui and Xiao-Liang Xie and Shi-Qi Liu and Zeng-Guang Hou}, title = {{O}nline {A}daptation via {D}ual-{S}tage {A}lignment and {S}elf-{S}upervision for {F}ast-{C}alibration {B}rain-{C}omputer {I}nterfaces}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2509.19403}, note = {arXiv:2509.19403v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIndividual differences in brain activity hinder the online application of electroencephalogram (EEG)-based brain computer interface (BCI) systems. To overcome this limitation, this study proposes an online adaptation algorithm for unseen subjects via dual-stage alignment and self-supervision. The alignment process begins by applying Euclidean alignment in the EEG data space and then updates batch normalization statistics in the representation space. Moreover, a self-supervised loss is designed to update the decoder. The loss is computed by soft pseudo-labels derived from the decoder as a proxy for the unknown ground truth, and is calibrated by Shannon entropy to facilitate self-supervised training. Experiments across five public datasets and seven decoders show the proposed algorithm can be integrated seamlessly regardless of BCI paradigm and decoder architecture. In each iteration, the decoder is updated with a single online trial, which yields average accuracy gains of 4.9% on steady-state visual evoked potentials (SSVEP) and 3.6% on motor imagery. These results support fast-calibration operation and show that the proposed algorithm has great potential for BCI applications.\n\n  * Overview of LifeCLEF Plant Identification task 2020\n\nHerve Goeau, Pierre Bonnet, Alexis Joly\n\nSep 25 2025 cs.CV arXiv:2509.19402v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2509.19402, author = {Herve Goeau and Pierre Bonnet and Alexis Joly}, title = {{O}verview of {L}ife{CLEF} {P}lant {I}dentification task 2020}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2509.19402}, note = {arXiv:2509.19402v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nAutomated identification of plants has improved considerably thanks to the recent progress in deep learning and the availability of training data with more and more photos in the field. However, this profusion of data only concerns a few tens of thousands of species, mostly located in North America and Western Europe, much less in the richest regions in terms of biodiversity such as tropical countries. On the other hand, for several centuries, botanists have collected, catalogued and systematically stored plant specimens in herbaria, particularly in tropical regions, and the recent efforts by the biodiversity informatics community made it possible to put millions of digitized sheets online. The LifeCLEF 2020 Plant Identification challenge (or \"PlantCLEF 2020\") was designed to evaluate to what extent automated identification on the flora of data deficient regions can be improved by the use of herbarium collections. It is based on a dataset of about 1,000 species mainly focused on the South America's Guiana Shield, an area known to have one of the greatest diversity of plants in the world. The challenge was evaluated as a cross-domain classification task where the training set consist of several hundred thousand herbarium sheets and few thousand of photos to enable learning a mapping between the two domains. The test set was exclusively composed of photos in the field. This paper presents the resources and assessments of the conducted evaluation, summarizes the approaches and systems employed by the participating research groups, and provides an analysis of the main outcomes.\n\n  * SpellerSSL: Self-Supervised Learning with P300 Aggregation for Speller BCIs\n\nJiazhen Hong, Geoff Mackellar, Soheila Ghane\n\nSep 25 2025 eess.SP cs.LG arXiv:2509.19401v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2509.19401, author = {Jiazhen Hong and Geoff Mackellar and Soheila Ghane}, title = {{S}peller{SSL}: {S}elf-{S}upervised {L}earning with {P}300 {A}ggregation for {S}peller {BCI}s}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2509.19401}, note = {arXiv:2509.19401v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n  * Meow: End-to-End Outline Writing for Automatic Academic Survey\n\nZhaoyu Ma, Yuan Shan, Jiahao Zhao, Nan Xu, Lei Wang\n\nSep 25 2025 cs.CL cs.AI arXiv:2509.19370v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2509.19370, author = {Zhaoyu Ma and Yuan Shan and Jiahao Zhao and Nan Xu and Lei Wang}, title = {{M}eow: {E}nd-to-{E}nd {O}utline {W}riting for {A}utomatic {A}cademic {S}urvey}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2509.19370}, note = {arXiv:2509.19370v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nAs academic paper publication numbers grow exponentially, conducting in-depth surveys with LLMs automatically has become an inevitable trend. Outline writing, which aims to systematically organize related works, is critical for automated survey generation. Yet existing automatic survey methods treat outline writing as mere workflow steps in the overall pipeline. Such template-based workflows produce outlines that lack in-depth understanding of the survey topic and fine-grained styles. To address these limitations, we propose Meow, the first metadata-driven outline writing framework that produces organized and faithful outlines efficiently. Specifically, we first formulate outline writing as an end-to-end task that generates hierarchical structured outlines from paper metadata. We then curate a high-quality dataset of surveys from arXiv, bioRxiv, and medRxiv, and establish systematic evaluation metrics for outline quality assessment. Finally, we employ a two-stage training approach combining supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning. Our 8B reasoning model demonstrates strong performance with high structural fidelity and stylistic coherence.\n\n  * SLM-Based Agentic AI with P-C-G: Optimized for Korean Tool Use\n\nChanghyun Jeon, Jinhee Park, Jungwoo Choi, Keonwoo Kim, Jisu Kim, Minji Hong\n\nSep 25 2025 cs.CL cs.AI arXiv:2509.19369v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2509.19369, author = {Changhyun Jeon and Jinhee Park and Jungwoo Choi and Keonwoo Kim and Jisu Kim and Minji Hong}, title = {{SLM}-{B}ased {A}gentic {AI} with {P}-{C}-{G}: {O}ptimized for {K}orean {T}ool {U}se}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2509.19369}, note = {arXiv:2509.19369v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe propose a small-scale language model (SLM) based agent architecture, Planner-Caller-Generator (P-C-G), optimized for Korean tool use. P-C-G separates planning, calling, and generation by role: the Planner produces an initial batch plan with limited on-demand replanning; the Caller returns a normalized call object after joint schema-value validation; and the Generator integrates tool outputs to produce the final answer. We apply a Korean-first value policy to reduce execution failures caused by frequent Korean-to-English code switching in Korean settings. Evaluation assumes Korean queries and Korean tool/parameter specifications; it covers single-chain, multi-chain, missing-parameters, and missing-functions scenarios, and is conducted via an LLM-as-a-Judge protocol averaged over five runs under a unified I/O interface. Results show that P-C-G delivers competitive tool-use accuracy and end-to-end quality while reducing tokens and maintaining acceptable latency, indicating that role-specialized SLMs are a cost-effective alternative for Korean tool-use agents.\n\n  * Pipeline Parallelism is All You Need for Optimized Early-Exit Based Self-Speculative Decoding\n\nRuanjun Li, Ziheng Liu, Yuanming Shi, Jiawei Shao, Chi Zhang, Xuelong Li\n\nSep 25 2025 cs.CL cs.AI arXiv:2509.19368v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2509.19368, author = {Ruanjun Li and Ziheng Liu and Yuanming Shi and Jiawei Shao and Chi Zhang and Xuelong Li}, title = {{P}ipeline {P}arallelism is {A}ll {Y}ou {N}eed for {O}ptimized {E}arly-{E}xit {B}ased {S}elf-{S}peculative {D}ecoding}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2509.19368}, note = {arXiv:2509.19368v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSLM-Based Agentic AI with P-C-G: Optimized for Korean Tool Use (https://paperreading.club/page?id=340563)\nciteturn8720search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19369\nResults show that P-C-G delivers competitive tool-use accuracy and end-to-end quality while reducing tokens and maintaining acceptable latency, indicating that role-specialized SLMs are a cost-effective alternative for Korean tool-use agents.\n\n##### Abstract (translated)\n\n我们提出了一种基于角色的小规模语言模型（SLM）代理架构，名为规划者-调用者-生成器（P-C-G），该架构专为韩语工具使用进行了优化。在P-C-G架构中，通过角色分工来分别执行计划、调用和生成任务：规划者负责产生初始批次计划，并进行有限的按需重规划；调用者返回经过规范化处理且经过联合模式-值验证后的调用对象；生成器则整合工具输出以生成最终答案。我们采用优先考虑韩语的价值策略，以此减少在韩语环境中频繁出现的韩英代码切换导致的执行失败问题。 评估假设使用韩语查询以及韩语工具和参数规格，并涵盖了单一链路、多链路、缺少参数和缺失功能等场景。该评估通过一个统一输入输出接口，在五次运行中采用大规模语言模型作为裁判进行平均评分来完成。实验结果显示，P-C-G架构在保证工具使用准确性和端到端质量的同时，减少了所需令牌数并保持了可接受的延迟时间，表明针对韩语工具使用的代理而言，角色专门化的SLM是一种成本效益高的替代方案。\n\n##### URL\n\nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19369\n\n##### PDF [Button: Copy]\n\nhttps://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.19369.pdf--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n#연구 #논문 #기술 #에이전틱 #데이터베이스 | 정지환 (https://kr.linkedin.com/posts/jihwanjeong_%EC%97%B0%EA%B5%AC-%EB%85%BC%EB%AC%B8-%EA%B8%B0%EC%88%A0-activity-7379359158871138306-PaE4)\nciteturn8720search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; P-C-G(기획-호출-생성) 모델과 관련한 'SLM-Based Agentic AI with P–C–G: Optimized for Korean Tool Use' 논문은 아래 링크에서 볼 수 있습니다. https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19369\n이는 마치 유능한 리서치 전문가가 단지 키워드만으로 검색하는 것을 넘어, 질문의 숨은 의도와 맥락을 파악하고 관련성이 높은 파생 질문들을 예상해 가장 정확한 답변을 찾아낼 수 있도록 준비하는 것입니다. 2편의 논문을 통해 한글과컴퓨터가 연구 중인 혁신적인 ‘에이전틱(Agentic) AI’ 기술을 선보임으로써, 미래 AI 기술 기업으로서의 비전과 성장 가능성을 알리는 의미 있는 계기가 될 것입니다.\n\n  *   * `` ``\n\n정지환 8개월\n\n검색 증강 생성(RAG) 성능과 관련한 'Chunk Knowledge Generation Model for Enhanced Information Retrieval: A Multi-task Learning Approach' 논문은 아래 링크에서 볼 수 있습니다. https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15658\n\n추천\n\n정지환 8개월\n\nP-C-G(기획-호출-생성) 모델과 관련한 'SLM-Based Agentic AI with P–C–G: Optimized for Korean Tool Use' 논문은 아래 링크에서 볼 수 있습니다. https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19369\n\n추천--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeiqiang Lou's research works (https://www.researchgate.net/scientific-contributions/Weiqiang-Lou-2325568148)\nciteturn8720search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; September 2025 ... This paper presents ByteRobust, a large-scale GPU infrastructure management system tailored for robust and stable training of LLMs.\n\nOctober 2025\n\n·\n\n26 Reads\n\n·\n\n13 Citations\n\nBorui Wan\n\n·\n\nGaohong Liu\n\n·\n\nZuquan Song\n\n·\n\n[...]\n\n·\n\nLiang Xiang\n\n+2\n\nRobust LLM Training Infrastructure at ByteDance\n\nPreprint\n\nFile available\n\nSeptember 2025\n\n·\n\n98 Reads\n\nBorui Wan\n\n·\n\nGaohong Liu\n\n·\n\nZuquan Song\n\n·\n\n[...]\n\n·\n\nLiang Xiang\n\nThe training scale of large language models (LLMs) has reached tens of thousands of GPUs and is still continuously expanding, enabling faster learning of larger models. Accompanying the expansion of the resource scale is the prevalence of failures (CUDA error, NaN values, job hang, etc.), which poses significant challenges to training stability. Any large-scale LLM training infrastructure should strive for minimal training interruption, efficient fault diagnosis, and effective failure tolerance to enable highly efficient continuous training. This paper presents ByteRobust, a large-scale GPU infrastructure management system tailored for robust and stable training of LLMs. It exploits the uniqueness of LLM training process and gives top priorities to detecting and recovering failures in a routine manner. Leveraging parallelisms and characteristics of LLM training, ByteRobust enables high-capacity fault tolerance, prompt fault demarcation, and localization with an effective data-driven approach, comprehensively ensuring continuous and efficient training of LLM tasks. ByteRobust is deployed on a production GPU platform with over 200,000 GPUs and achieves 97% ETTR for a three-month training job on 9,600 GPUs.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\nDownload\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n이제는 여행지나 교통편 검색부터 알아보기 귀찮고 복잡한 일까지 AI가 대신할 수 있는 세상입니다. 하지만 세상에 공짜는 없습니다. (https://kr.linkedin.com/pulse/%EC%9D%B4%EC%A0%9C%EB%8A%94-%EC%97%AC%ED%96%89%EC%A7%80%EB%82%98-%EA%B5%90%ED%86%B5%ED%8E%B8-%EA%B2%80%EC%83%89%EB%B6%80%ED%84%B0-%EC%95%8C%EC%95%84%EB%B3%B4%EA%B8%B0-%EA%B7%80%EC%B0%AE%EA%B3%A0-%EB%B3%B5%EC%9E%A1%ED%95%9C-%EC%9D%BC%EA%B9%8C%EC%A7%80-ai%EA%B0%80-%EB%8C%80%EC%8B%A0%ED%95%A0-%EC%88%98-%EC%9E%88%EB%8A%94-%EC%84%B8%EC%83%81%EC%9E%85%EB%8B%88%EB%8B%A4-%ED%95%98%EC%A7%80%EB%A7%8C-%EC%84%B8%EC%83%81%EC%97%90-%EA%B3%B5%EC%A7%9C%EB%8A%94-uv41c)\nciteturn8720search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 4 months ago; P-C-G(기획-호출-생성) 모델과 관련한 'SLM-Based Agentic AI with P–C–G: Optimized for Korean Tool Use' 논문은 아래 링크에서 볼 수 있습니다. https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19369\n하지만 분명한 것은 이번 연구가 AI 에이전트의 대중화를 앞당길 중요한 이정표가 될 수 있다는 사실입니다. AI 경쟁의 초점이 “누가 더 큰 모델을 만들었는가?”에서 “누가 더 효율적으로 설계했는가?”로 옮겨가고 있습니다. 한컴은 비용과 품질을 모두 잡을 수 있는 구조적 혁신으로, ‘작지만, 강한 AI 에이전트’ 시대를 열 것입니다.\n\n`` `` `` `` ``\n\n한글과컴퓨터 7개월\n\nP-C-G(기획-호출-생성) 모델과 관련한 'SLM-Based Agentic AI with P–C–G: Optimized for Korean Tool Use' 논문은 아래 링크에서 볼 수 있습니다. https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19369--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArxiver (https://arxiver.lazybrains.com/author/2002928)\nciteturn8720search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; arXiv:2509.19369\n\nWe propose a small-scale language model (SLM) based agent architecture, Planner-Caller-Generator (P-C-G), optimized for Korean tool use. P-C-G separates planning, calling, and generation by role: the Planner produces an initial batch plan with limited on-demand replanning; the Caller returns a normalized call object after joint schema-value validation; and the Generator integrates tool outputs to produce the final answer. We apply a Korean-first value policy to reduce execution failures caused by frequent Korean-to-English code switching in Korean settings. Evaluation assumes Korean queries and Korean tool/parameter specifications; it covers single-chain, multi-chain, missing-parameters, and missing-functions scenarios, and is conducted via an LLM-as-a-Judge protocol averaged over five runs under a unified I/O interface. Results show that P-C-G delivers competitive tool-use accuracy and end-to-end quality while reducing tokens and maintaining acceptable latency, indicating that role-specialized SLMs are a cost-effective alternative for Korean tool-use agents.\n\narXiv:2509.19369\nCreated: 2025-09-19 Updated: 2025-09-25--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nYuhan Li's research works (https://www.researchgate.net/scientific-contributions/Yuhan-Li-2325590096)\nciteturn8720search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; September 2025 ... This paper presents ByteRobust, a large-scale GPU infrastructure management system tailored for robust and stable training of LLMs.\n\nOctober 2025\n\n·\n\n22 Reads\n\n·\n\n6 Citations\n\nBorui Wan\n\n·\n\nGaohong Liu\n\n·\n\nZuquan Song\n\n·\n\n[...]\n\n·\n\nLiang Xiang\n\n* * *\n\n+2\n\nRobust LLM Training Infrastructure at ByteDance\n\n  * Preprint\n  * File available\n\nSeptember 2025\n\n·\n\n70 Reads\n\nBorui Wan\n\n·\n\nGaohong Liu\n\n·\n\nZuquan Song\n\n·\n\n[...]\n\n·\n\nLiang Xiang\n\nThe training scale of large language models (LLMs) has reached tens of thousands of GPUs and is still continuously expanding, enabling faster learning of larger models. Accompanying the expansion of the resource scale is the prevalence of failures (CUDA error, NaN values, job hang, etc.), which poses significant challenges to training stability. Any large-scale LLM training infrastructure should strive for minimal training interruption, efficient fault diagnosis, and effective failure tolerance to enable highly efficient continuous training. This paper presents ByteRobust, a large-scale GPU infrastructure management system tailored for robust and stable training of LLMs. It exploits the uniqueness of LLM training process and gives top priorities to detecting and recovering failures in a routine manner. Leveraging parallelisms and characteristics of LLM training, ByteRobust enables high-capacity fault tolerance, prompt fault demarcation, and localization with an effective data-driven approach, comprehensively ensuring continuous and efficient training of LLM tasks. ByteRobust is deployed on a production GPU platform with over 200,000 GPUs and achieves 97% ETTR for a three-month training job on 9,600 GPUs.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\nDownload\n\n## Citations (1)\n\n* * *\n\n> ... In the context of LLM training, vendors such as Google [21], Meta [22], ByteDance [23], and DeepSeek [24] have highlighted the growing importance of addressing SDCs. Google's Gemini models, for instance, encountered SDCrelated disruptions approximately every one to two weeks [21], while Meta reported six such incidents during a 54-day training run [22]. ...\n> \n> Reference:\n> \n> Exploring Silent Data Corruption as a Reliability Challenge in LLM Training\n\nRobust LLM Training Infrastructure at ByteDance\n\n  * Citing Conference Paper\n  * October 2025\n\nBorui Wan\n\n·\n\nGaohong Liu\n\n·\n\nZuquan Song\n\n·\n\n[...]\n\n·\n\nLiang Xiang\n\n\n## Top co-authors\n\n* * *\n\nImage\n\nChuan wu\n\nNational Taiwan Normal University\n\nImage\n\nHaibin Lin\n\nAmazon\n\nImage\n\nGuangming Sheng\n\nThe University of Hong Kong\n\nImage\n\nShuguang Wang\n\nImage\n\nGaohong Liu\n\nImage\n\nJinxin Chi\n\nImage\n\nQinlong Wang\n\nImage\n\nKaihua Jiang\n\nImage\n\nJun Wang\n\nImage\n\nZixian Du\n\n## Publication stats\n\n* * *\n\nCitations\n\n* * *\n\n6\n\nBrowse more researchers--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://vpn.luca-server.com/px/aHR0cHM6Ly9hcnhpdi5vcmcvc2VhcmNoL2NzP3NlYXJjaHR5cGU9YXV0aG9yJnF1ZXJ5PVpob25nJTJDK0I)\nciteturn8720search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; This paper presents ByteRobust, a large-scale GPU infrastructure management system tailored for robust and stable training of LLMs. ... Submitted 20 October, 2025; v1 submitted 19 September, 2025; originally announced September 2025.\nIt exploits the uniqueness of LLM training process and gives top priorities to detecting and recovering failures in a routine manner. Leveraging parallelisms and characteristics of LLM training, ByteRobust enables high-capacity fault tolerance, prompt fault demarcation, and localization with an effective data-driven approach, comprehensively ensuring continuous and efficient training of LLM tasks. ByteRobust is deployed on a production GPU platform and achieves 97% ETTR for a three-month training job on 9,600 GPUs. △ Less\n\nSubmitted 20 October, 2025; v1 submitted 19 September, 2025; originally announced September 2025.\n\n  28. arXiv:2509.14544  [pdf, ps, other] \n\ncs.CV\n\nAssociation and Consolidation: Evolutionary Memory-Enhanced Incremental Multi-View Clustering\n\nAuthors: Zisen Kong, Bo Zhong, Pengyuan Li, Dongxia Chang, Yiming Wang, Yongyong Chen\n\nAbstract: Incremental multi-view clustering aims to achieve stable clustering results while addressing the stability-plasticity dilemma (SPD) in view-incremental scenarios. Finally, we propose a knowledge consolidation module to progressively refine short-term knowledge into stable long-term memory using temporal tensors, thereby ensuring model stability. By integrating these modules, EMIMC achieves strong knowledge retention capabilities in scenarios with growing views. Extensive experiments demonstrate that EMIMC exhibits remarkable advantages over existing state-of-the-art methods. △ Less\n\nSubmitted 11 November, 2025; v1 submitted 17 September, 2025; originally announced September 2025.\n\nComments: Submitted to CVPR2026\n\n  29. arXiv:2509.02544  [pdf, ps, other] \n\ncs.AI cs.CL cs.CV cs.HC\n\nUI-TARS-2 Technical Report: Advancing GUI Agent with Multi-Turn Reinforcement Learning\n\nAuthors: Haoming Wang, Haoyang Zou, Huatong Song, Jiazhan Feng, Junjie Fang, Junting Lu, Longxiang Liu, Qinyu Luo, Shihao Liang, Shijue Huang, Wanjun Zhong, Yining Ye, Yujia Qin, Yuwen Xiong, Yuxin Song, Zhiyong Wu, Aoyan Li, Bo Li, Chen Dun, Chong Liu, Daoguang Zan, Fuxing Leng, Hanbin Wang, Hao Yu, Haobin Chen , et al. (87 additional authors not shown)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSLM-Based Agentic AI with P-C-G: Optimized for Korean Tool Use (https://chatpaper.com/pt/chatpaper/paper/191504)\nciteturn8720search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Figura 4: O formato de entrada/saída do Chamador](https://chatdoc-arxiv.oss-us-west-1.aliyuncs.com/images/arxiv/2509.19369/figure_4.png?\nEsse formato é mostrado na Figura 4 e permite integração perfeita com diversas ferramentas. O Gerador, por sua vez, opera em um template de chat mínimo e usa apenas as interações essenciais: sistema (metadados), usuário (consulta), assistente (chamadas de ferramentas) e ferramenta (resultados). Essa estrutura leve, representada na Figura 5, permite uma gestão eficiente do contexto e geração de respostas focadas. O Gerador integra as saídas das ferramentas, interpreta os resultados e os reformata em respostas em linguagem natural que correspondem à intenção do usuário e ao formato solicitado (por exemplo, tabelas, resumos). Ao separar a integração de resultados do planejamento e execução, o Gerador evita alucinações e garante uma base factual. Juntos, esses módulos impõem uma rigorosa fidelidade de dados e consistência linguística, tornando o sistema robusto em ambientes de serviços coreanos do mundo real e em múltiplas etapas. ![Figura 4: O formato de entrada/saída do Chamador](https://chatdoc-arxiv.oss-us-west-1.aliyuncs.com/images/arxiv/2509.19369/figure_4.png?AWSAccessKeyId=LTAI5t6b2G8eTtEBczAMwjhc&Signature=l%2B1HjZoKxmZxPkV3zkvaBQ9P3Jw%3D&Expires=1779747008) **Figura 4: O formato de entrada/saída do Chamador** ![Figura 5: Estrutura mínima de mensagem para uso de ferramentas](https://chatdoc-arxiv.oss-us-west-1.aliyuncs.com/images/arxiv/2509.19369/figure_5.png?AWSAccessKeyId=LTAI5t6b2G8eTtEBczAMwjhc&Signature=e%2FRCTj7QBAz%2BPCN2gY8lrlOQsiM%3D&Expires=1779747008) **Figura 5: Estrutura mínima de mensagem para uso de ferramentas** ## Estrutura de Avaliação e Resultados Esta seção descreve a configuração experimental, o design do conjunto de dados e as principais descobertas da avaliação da arquitetura P–C–G.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMenghan Yu's research works (https://www.researchgate.net/scientific-contributions/Menghan-Yu-2312968827)\nciteturn8720search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; September 2025 ... This paper presents ByteRobust, a large-scale GPU infrastructure management system tailored for robust and stable training of LLMs.\n\nPreprint\n\nFile available\n\nSeptember 2025\n\n·\n\n102 Reads\n\nBorui Wan\n\n·\n\nGaohong Liu\n\n·\n\nZuquan Song\n\n·\n\n[...]\n\n·\n\nLiang Xiang\n\nThe training scale of large language models (LLMs) has reached tens of thousands of GPUs and is still continuously expanding, enabling faster learning of larger models. Accompanying the expansion of the resource scale is the prevalence of failures (CUDA error, NaN values, job hang, etc.), which poses significant challenges to training stability. Any large-scale LLM training infrastructure should strive for minimal training interruption, efficient fault diagnosis, and effective failure tolerance to enable highly efficient continuous training. This paper presents ByteRobust, a large-scale GPU infrastructure management system tailored for robust and stable training of LLMs. It exploits the uniqueness of LLM training process and gives top priorities to detecting and recovering failures in a routine manner. Leveraging parallelisms and characteristics of LLM training, ByteRobust enables high-capacity fault tolerance, prompt fault demarcation, and localization with an effective data-driven approach, comprehensively ensuring continuous and efficient training of LLM tasks. ByteRobust is deployed on a production GPU platform with over 200,000 GPUs and achieves 97% ETTR for a three-month training job on 9,600 GPUs.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\nDownload\n\nBootSeer: Analyzing and Mitigating Initialization Bottlenecks in Large-Scale LLM Training\n\nPreprint\n\nJuly 2025\n\n·\n\n7 Reads\n\nRui Li\n\n·\n\nXiaoyun Zhi\n\n·\n\nJinxin Chi\n\n·\n\n[...]\n\n·\n\nGene Cooperman\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) have become a cornerstone of modern AI, driving breakthroughs in natural language processing and expanding into multimodal jobs involving images, audio, and video. As with most computational software, it is important to distinguish between ordinary runtime performance and startup overhead. Prior research has focused on runtime performance: improving training efficiency and stability. This work focuses instead on the increasingly critical issue of startup overhead in training: the delay before training jobs begin execution. Startup overhead is particularly important in large, industrial-scale LLMs, where failures occur more frequently and multiple teams operate in iterative update-debug cycles. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRobust LLM Training Infrastructure at ByteDance | hgpu.org (https://hgpu.org/?p=30265)\nciteturn8720search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last week; This paper presents ByteRobust, a large-scale GPU infrastructure management system tailored for robust and stable training of LLMs. ... September 28, 2025 by hgpu\n\n   year={2025},\n\n   eprint={2509.16293},\n\n   archivePrefix={arXiv},\n\n   primaryClass={cs.LG},\n\n   url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16293}\n\n}\n\nDownload (PDF)    View    Source   \n\n2617\n\nviews\n\nThe training scale of large language models (LLMs) has reached tens of thousands of GPUs and is still continuously expanding, enabling faster learning of larger models. Accompanying the expansion of the resource scale is the prevalence of failures (CUDA error, NaN values, job hang, etc.), which poses significant challenges to training stability. Any large-scale LLM training infrastructure should strive for minimal training interruption, efficient fault diagnosis, and effective failure tolerance to enable highly efficient continuous training. This paper presents ByteRobust, a large-scale GPU infrastructure management system tailored for robust and stable training of LLMs. It exploits the uniqueness of LLM training process and gives top priorities to detecting and recovering failures in a routine manner. Leveraging parallelisms and characteristics of LLM training, ByteRobust enables high-capacity fault tolerance, prompt fault demarcation, and localization with an effective data-driven approach, comprehensively ensuring continuous and efficient training of LLM tasks. ByteRobust is deployed on a production GPU platform with over 200,000 GPUs and achieves 97% ETTR for a three-month training job on 9,600 GPUs\n\nTags: AI, Computer science, CUDA, LLM, nVidia, nVidia L20\n\nSeptember 28, 2025 by hgpu"
